 a middle tile with many left, but it could be useful if I switch to chows. Given Seven Pairs, I just need pairs, so any single is equal. I'll discard the one with the most unshown tiles to increase drawing chance for others? Actually, for Seven Pairs, I want to keep tiles that have more unshown copies available to draw pairs. T4 has 4 unshown, highest among my singles. So I should keep T4. The least unshown? B7 has 1 unshown, but I don't have B7. Among mine, B2 has 3, T1 has 3, T2 has 3, T5 has 3, W6 has 2. W6 has only 2 unshown, so harder to pair. But I have only one W6, so discarding it reduces clutter. However, if I discard W6, I lose chance for a W6 pair. But with only 2 left, it's less likely. Also, W6 is a middle tile, not terminal, so less dangerous. \n\nI think discarding W6 is reasonable. It was previously discarded by me, so might be safe. But let's check history: I played W6 earlier, and previous player played W6 later. So W6 has been seen twice. There are 2 left, so possible but not high chance. Alternatively, T1 is a terminal, which might be more dangerous to discard because someone might need it for patterns. But terminal discards are common early. \n\nGiven the previous player has made chows with terminals (B5-B7 includes B7 terminal, T7-T9 includes T9 terminal), they might not need T1. I'll discard T1 as a terminal to potentially reduce danger? Actually, in Seven Pairs, terminals are fine as pairs. T1 could become a pair if I draw another. With 3 unshown, it's possible. I need to decide which single to discard to maximize flexibility. Since I have four pairs, I should keep suits that are more likely to pair. Honors (J1, J3) are good because they have fewer tiles in play? J1 has 3 unshown, J3 has 3 unshown. My singles are all suited. I'll discard B2 because it's a simple tile and has been discarded, so likely safe.\n\nBut let's think about defense: Previous player has chows in B and T, so they might be collecting B suits. Discarding B2 might feed them. Better to discard a tile in a suit they are less likely to need. They have B5-B7 chow, so they might be collecting B middles. B2 is low, maybe not. They also have T7-T9 chow, so high T. So T1 is low T, maybe safe. Actually, they discarded T1 earlier, so they probably don't need T1. So T1 is safe. I'll discard T1.\n\nReason: Pursuing Seven Pairs (24 points) is a strong and achievable goal given I already have four pairs. I need to maintain all pairs and discard singles strategically. Discarding T1, a terminal with some unshown copies, is safe based on discard history and doesn't hinder my Seven Pairs plan, as I can still form pairs with remaining singles. It also avoids feeding opponents' potential melds.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
