o make chows with T4,T5, or draw another T2 for a kong).  \\n\\n**Reason:**  \\nGiven the unseen tile counts:  \\n- J1: 2 left unseen (you hold 2, so if someone discards J1 now, it might be safe to take it to secure a pung and wind/dragon points).  \\n- T2: 3 unseen (you already have 3 T2; if you keep them concealed, you could eventually get a fourth for a concealed kong, scoring more).  \\n- T4, T5: several still unseen but limited.  \\n\\nIf you Peng J1:  \\n1. You get a guaranteed **Dragon Pung** pattern (2 points) and possibly **Prevalent Wind** or **Seat Wind** if relevant (J1 is Red Dragon, not wind).  \\n2. You speed up your hand toward ready state, but your remaining tiles are awkward: three T2 (which can\\u2019t make a chow) and T4,T5 (need one more tile to complete a chow).  \\n3. Discarding after Peng will be tricky: discarding T4 or T5 might give away safe tiles; discarding T2 would break a potential pung/kong.  \\n\\nIf you Pass:  \\n1. You keep your hand closed, preserving chance for **Concealed Kong** or **Concealed Pung** bonuses.  \\n2. You might draw a T3 or T6 next turn to combine with T4,T5 into a chow, improving flexibility.  \\n3. Risk: someone else might win before you complete your hand, given many melds are already exposed by others.  \\n\\nGiven the game stage (many tiles played, many melds exposed), speeding up might be better. Peng J1 gives you a known scoring pattern (Dragon Pung) and moves you to 3 exposed melds, leaving only 4 hand tiles to manage. That\\u2019s a reasonable aggressive move.  \\n\\n**Answer:** Peng J1\"}", "tile": "{\"system_prompt\": \"\\n    You are a Chinese Standard Mahjong (\\u56fd\\u6807\\u9ebb\\u5c06, or MCR Mahjong) player. It is a four-player game, and it is played with a basic set of 136 tiles with Chinese characters and symbols.\\n    Players draw and discard tiles in turn until they complete a winning hand with a 14th tile. The basic type of winning hand consists of four melds and a pair, while there exist w